#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566225 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "14cc6590-c2fd-5395-9481-0e5c0d9b8b8b" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Chain Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a507ce6-22ce-56f0-ba53-527362e98081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x^{3}+16xy^{2}-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565924}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a507ce6-22ce-56f0-ba53-527362e98081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8x^{3}+16xy^{2}-16x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565924} :answer-field/id #uuid "5406b9c4-8f08-5b5f-8ea0-96705a9a7215" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566141}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d7ebf34-a035-5c2e-ad76-841acaa3c221" :question/math-academy-id "q-168886" :question/problem "$\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the given function with respect to $x,$ treating all other variables as constants. \n\nFirst, we use the sum rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the constant multiple rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{4})+\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}{y}^{2})-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{2}) & =2\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4})+8{y}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2})-8\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4{x}^{3}+8{y}^{2}\\cdot 2x-8\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566263 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198411 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d912b296-6fd0-57c2-84e8-ca0b56549756" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of a Scalar Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6c2d85b-0791-5631-9a96-b310c9e733e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16x^{2}y+12y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6c2d85b-0791-5631-9a96-b310c9e733e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16x^{2}y+12y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565997} :answer-field/id #uuid "01c5dfcb-2472-5349-af19-428547c6b2a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566101}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7c3402d-fbe9-5875-8d06-b63e54fe220e" :question/math-academy-id "q-168887" :question/problem "${f}^{′}(x)=$$[\\,8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x$ \n{{field-1}}\n$]$" :question/worked-solution "For our scalar function, we have that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={(∇f)}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nEarlier, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x.\n$$\n\nThe partial derivative of $f$ with respect to $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-8{x}^{2}+6{y}^{2}-1)=16{x}^{2}y+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\,8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x\\, & \\,16{x}^{2}y+12y\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124647 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fb118f-c1cc-51fd-a8cc-c29cf705acfe" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Local Extrema of a Multivariable Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da8d5be4-4fd2-5cf8-ba70-65f748d32b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da8d5be4-4fd2-5cf8-ba70-65f748d32b36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566029} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d9a9cef-2038-557f-85dd-5cc5297a49b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566130}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "187224fe-1282-5974-b5d9-f692bdbb4202" :question/math-academy-id "q-168888" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d2132be4de3f505e5052ae3083b75ac8aceceed9f64bf62d128746a37a59a23.png)\n\nGiven a sketch of the graph of $z=f(x)$ above, which of the points ($A,$ $B,$ or $C$) is a local minimum of the function?\n\nA local minimum is the point \n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum point at $(a,b)$ if $f(x,y)\\ge f(a,b)$ for all points $(x,y)$ in a neighborhood of $(a,b).$ \n\nIn other words, we can take a neighborhood of $(x,y)$-values such that the point $(x,y,f(x,y))$ is the lowest point on the graph in the neighborhood.\n\nAmong the given options, such neighborhoods exist for the point $C.$ Hence, $C$ is a local minimum.\n\n![](images/a3/a389560d6217e86b82384443c5730168483a75a1759788ddde31ef64ad368adc.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566217 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124653 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "045854fc-9cad-5005-a306-9550848e25df" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Critical Points of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7500e34d-8118-54e7-bd9f-4a712248dfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7500e34d-8118-54e7-bd9f-4a712248dfb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565860} :answer-field/id #uuid "759d25c6-2087-5621-820c-1d640ea38f01" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566152} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a435757-c811-58a0-a8a9-6e8c5f74e36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a435757-c811-58a0-a8a9-6e8c5f74e36c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565692} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f97737c-ae6c-590f-a770-d33c927a54f7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566159} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55f70a89-bcfe-5aed-b870-1901d78733aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55f70a89-bcfe-5aed-b870-1901d78733aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565814} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa6b1fc8-4a0e-5fee-a4ba-3951cd3851a0" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566172}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2ed0a30c-70d1-57fa-8ba9-ce180a14b5a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-168889" :question/problem "The critical points of $f$ are$\\,$$(\\pm $ \n{{field-1}}\n$,$ \n{{field-2}}\n$)$ and $(0,$ \n{{field-3}}\n$).$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x & \\,16{x}^{2}y+12y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=0 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y}=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x=0 \\\\ 16{x}^{2}y+12y=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}8x({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2)=0 \\\\ 4y(4{x}^{2}+3)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $4{x}^{2}+3\\ne 0,$ from the second equation of the system, we obtain that\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8x({x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}+{0}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 8x({x}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\pm \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\nNote that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these points, they are also stationary points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566270 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124440 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ea31275-77bd-59c7-8e1d-96e2e4b252a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Mixed Second-Order Partial Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f25c240-f37d-534e-b187-1a4f8baf5379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16x^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f25c240-f37d-534e-b187-1a4f8baf5379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16x^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565704} :answer-field/id #uuid "a16bd320-c248-529b-b304-32eb2f00d3aa" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566169} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5410d817-daa4-5eb6-be7e-73949185627c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5410d817-daa4-5eb6-be7e-73949185627c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32xy" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565810} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac878e84-17ed-5ef9-87fa-14cf1f5f5446" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566173} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e172652-5fd0-5837-bbec-c3a968179703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24x^{2}+16y^{2}-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e172652-5fd0-5837-bbec-c3a968179703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24x^{2}+16y^{2}-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565798} :answer-field/id #uuid "b213367d-7142-5c18-b5f3-48ff7c0e4e22" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed3eb4a9-71c7-55f2-a5c3-dcaf4e7245dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-168890" :question/problem "Find the second partial derivatives of $f.$\n\n$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\,=$\n{{field-2}}\n\n$\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}=\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}\\,=$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=16{x}^{2}y+12y.\n$$\n\nComputing the second partial derivatives, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{x}^{3}+16x{y}^{2}-16x)=24{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-16 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(16{x}^{2}y+12y)=32xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(16{x}^{2}y+12y)=16{x}^{2}+12\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157424 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "037ff08b-3246-5eed-870b-b4951f319c96" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Hessian Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d6a5a41-e723-5f75-9166-621c4c0b5fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d6a5a41-e723-5f75-9166-621c4c0b5fea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-192" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565746} :answer-field/id #uuid "0189ae91-6ed6-5d12-a32f-c03fa82da30e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566100} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7d19913-62b2-5223-b8ec-7ca4b1fb9c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1408" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7d19913-62b2-5223-b8ec-7ca4b1fb9c97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1408" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565980} :answer-field/id #uuid "02a238bb-fadd-5461-8325-d873d6a724ae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566102}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "12e17457-b92e-5bbd-b375-4d92edbd2785" :question/math-academy-id "q-168891" :question/problem "Evaluate the Hessian determinant of $f$ at $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$\n\n$H(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)\\,=$ \n{{field-1}}\n\n$\\,H(0,0)\\,=$ \n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "We computed the second partial derivatives in part 5. So, the second derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}24{x}^{2}+16{y}^{2}-16 & 32xy \\\\ 32xy & 16{x}^{2}+12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n* Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=[\\begin{aligned}32 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 44\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=32\\cdot 44-0\\cdot 0=1\\,408.\n$$\n\n* Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-16 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=-16\\cdot 12-0\\cdot 0=-192.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186566244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124654 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ca319c5-9f62-51e3-b670-611fc8a8daaa" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a Critical Point Using the Second Partial Derivatives Test"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a990f136-6c72-5666-8278-bf851d95e098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565947}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a990f136-6c72-5666-8278-bf851d95e098" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565947} :answer-field/id #uuid "20dd0839-d52e-5270-a193-0c36a7580e1d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566118} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7621c0a7-630f-5c98-b4b9-5bd1e81050ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7621c0a7-630f-5c98-b4b9-5bd1e81050ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value ">" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565861} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6453212-afd5-5de3-8be7-5d403d904e1f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566178} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a467ab84-a2e1-51f1-bdc6-7daaba2f65af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a467ab84-a2e1-51f1-bdc6-7daaba2f65af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565941} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee637896-1be0-5705-861b-4b0376c0f76f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75a986c0-75fe-5567-a170-fed953f3d35d" :question/math-academy-id "q-168892" :question/problem "Classify the critical points using the second partial derivatives test.\n\n$(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ are local minima since $\\,H(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$\n{{field-1}}\n$0\\,\\,$ and $\\,\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$\n{{field-2}}\n$0$\n\n$(0,0)$ is a saddle point since $\\,\\,H(0,0)$\n{{field-3}}\n$0$" :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n* If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n  * if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$ \n\n  * if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n* If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n* If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nIn part 4, we found the critical points $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ and $(0,0).$ Their corresponding Hessian determinants and the second partial derivatives at these points were computed in part 6.\n\nWith that in mind, let's classify our critical points:\n\n* $(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)$ are local minima of $f$ since $H(\\pm \\sqrt{2},0)=1\\,408>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\pm \\sqrt{3},0)=32>0.$\n\n* $(0,0)$ is a saddle point since $H(0,0)=-192<0.$"}]}
